308380126953125, -0.01406097412109375, -0.0220489501953125, 0.00731658935546875, -0.0238494873046875, -0.0033416748046875, -0.00424957275390625, 0.011627197265625, 0.004047393798828125, 0.01311492919921875, -0.0175018310546875, 0.00722503662109375, 0.0231475830078125, 0.0163421630859375, -0.0013828277587890625, -0.0271148681640625, -0.0004584789276123047, -0.0076751708984375, 0.0150909423828125, -0.0193023681640625, -0.0010938644409179688, -0.007518768310546875, -0.02728271484375, 0.00411224365234375, 0.004364013671875, -0.00453948974609375, -0.007152557373046875, 0.032196044921875, 0.01513671875, 0.0170440673828125, -0.0197601318359375, 0.0010766983032226562, 0.01387786865234375, -0.0008106231689453125, -0.0009965896606445312, 0.001438140869140625, -0.022796630859375, 0.006755828857421875, -0.0168304443359375, -0.00450897216796875, -0.0153656005859375, -0.0254669189453125, 0.031890869140625, -0.04852294921875, 0.00197601318359375, -0.0261993408203125, -0.029693603515625, 0.0216522216796875, -0.031005859375, 0.00013124942779541016, -0.0115509033203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work addresses the following research questions: (1) How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order? (2) What factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved?\n\n**Background.** We focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes, and (b) Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA), which maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons. These methods have been evaluated primarily on fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order.\n\n**Methods.** We conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of EMAA and SRLA along two axes: (1) running each agent on five independent seeds to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks (drawn from a suite of 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks in simulated environments) to assess the effect of task order. Environments include both text-based and embodied simulation platforms. For memory analysis, we manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for specificity, relevance, and completeness of stored information. This process involves two independent annotators and qualitative coding tools.\n\n**Results.** Our experiments reveal two key findings that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: across five runs, standard deviation in final task success rates ranged from 8% to 15%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to catastrophic forgetting or spurious memory entries. Second, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: in some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility: memory entries often lack sufficient detail to generalize across task variations, and ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories. To validate this, we incorporate more detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback into the memory construction process. This intervention partially closes the performance gap, reducing average degradation from 20% to 10% in shuffled task orders, but significant gaps still remain.\n\n**Discussion.** These persistent gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient exploration, or unmodeled environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Future work should investigate these possibilities, for example by analyzing retrieval failures or systematically varying environment randomness.\n\n**Recommendations.** We advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including reporting results across multiple random seeds, systematically varying task order, and providing detailed breakdowns of failure cases. For effective human oversight, we recommend developing interfaces that allow users to audit and edit agent memories, and incorporating human-in-the-loop feedback to catch underspecified or misleading entries before they propagate.\n\nIn summary, our work highlights the need for transparent, robust evaluation and oversight in memory-based self-improving agents, and provides concrete steps toward more reliable deployment in complex environments."}}